// gear.ron — blades, tanks, resupply. Economy instead of cooldowns (init.md §1).
// ⚠️ UNTUNED, see game.ron.
(
    blades: (
        start_pairs: 5,
        // **What one landed hit costs the pair in your hands**, in sharpness (1.0 = fresh).
        // Read by `blades::cut::wear_of` since 2026-08-13 — before that this number had no
        // reader anywhere in `src/`, `Blades` was monotone upward, and the racks of the
        // headquarters handed back something nothing had ever taken (`FINDINGS.md` FIND-075).
        //
        // 0.12 is the **cortex** cut: 8 clean napes out of one pair, plus the ninth that
        // finishes it. Only a hit that is actually reported costs anything — a touch under
        // `min_speed_m_s` writes no `TitanHit` and does nothing to the titan, and a cost the
        // player cannot see the effect of is a cost he cannot learn from.
        wear_per_hit: 0.12,
        // **The graze costs LESS than the cut, and that direction is measured, not assumed.**
        // A pass that ends in a nape reports `[Torso, Cortex]` and always will: every titan is
        // wider than his own neck, so the blade meets the shoulder on the way in
        // (`tests/combat.rs::f030_the_cortex_wins_over_the_body_it_hides_in`). A torso factor
        // above 1.0 would therefore tax every successful kill for the geometry of the titan's
        // body rather than for the player's imprecision — the game charging you for winning,
        // which is the cooldown-shaped design the bible rejects.
        //
        // So the deep cut through hide, flesh and nape is what dulls steel; skidding off a
        // hardened flank is a scrape. One kill costs 0.12 + 0.06 = 0.18, so 5.5 kills per pair
        // and about 33 out of a full harness (five spares plus the pair in hand).
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED — see `docs/FINDINGS.md` FIND-079 for the two open tuning questions.
        wear_torso_factor: 0.5,
        // Damage comes out of SPEED: a slash from standing still scratches,
        // the same slash out of 30 m/s kills (init.md §1). The formula stands
        // here, not in Rust.
        damage_per_m_s: 1.4,
        min_speed_m_s: 8.0,

        // The blade's geometry and its swing. ⚠️ UNTUNED, all six invented by the
        // main head on 2026-08-09.
        //
        // **`reach_m` decides whether a cut lands at all** and did not exist until
        // 2026-08-09. It stood at 1.6 m — "an arm's length, not a lance" — until
        // 2026-08-20, when a sweep measured what that costs.
        //
        // 🔴 **This is the single number that decides how many kinds are killable, and
        // it is not `cortex_radius_m`.** The width of the band a nape can be cut from is
        //
        //     reach_m + thickness_m + cortex_radius_m − body_radius − player_radius
        //
        // and `body_radius` is `scale.ron: width_fraction 0.25 × height_m / 2`, which
        // grows with the size class about three times faster than any nape does. At 1.6
        // that band was **negative on two of the seven spawnable kinds**: measured
        // 2026-08-20, `scripts/f030-hitbox.txt` at a 0.60 m cushion killed 2 of 7, and a
        // lurker could not be killed at ANY lateral offset from 0.00 m to 0.70 m of air
        // — 8 passes, 8 torso hits, not one cortex (`docs/FINDINGS.md` FIND-147).
        //
        // 2.0 is what the same sweep needs: the binding kind is the lurker at
        // `1.75 + 0.35 + 0.60 + 0.13 − 0.12 − 0.77 = 1.94 m`, where the 0.13 is the
        // distance his own turn carries the nape sideways during the swing.
        //
        // **This is the reference's own lever, and it is the only one it pulled twice.**
        // AoT:R grew the PLAYER's slash hitbox and paid for it out of the target —
        // *"Increased the size of the slash hitbox overall (I reduced the nape and eyes
        // hitbox a bit for this)"*, then *"Increased player slash hitbox size by 5%"* —
        // and it never grew a nape to fix a miss (`docs/gameplay/references.md`).
        //
        // ⚠️ **Two prices, and both are real.** First: 2.0 m of cast against a drawn pair
        // that is **0.93 m** of steel (`FIND-127`), so the picture now under-promises by
        // **1.07 m** instead of 0.67. The honest fix is a longer blade model and it is
        // not this file's. Second: reach is direction-blind — it lengthens the segment
        // towards the titan's face exactly as much as towards his nape, and at 1.6 the
        // front pass of `q030_the_nape_is_cut_from_behind_and_not_from_the_front` was
        // only 0.080 m from landing. **That is why `titan.ron: cortex_half_angle_deg`
        // had to land in the same change**; without the gate this line alone would cut a
        // husk's nape from straight in front of him by 0.32 m.
        reach_m: 2.0,
        // **The swept capsule's radius — the thickness of the slash, not of the steel.**
        // Never zero: a zero-radius sweep is a ray, and a ray between two ticks at
        // 75 m/s threads a 0.46 m cortex.
        //
        // 0.12 -> 0.30 on 2026-08-20, and this is the lever that answers the axis
        // NOBODY had looked at. `reach_m` decides how far sideways the cut goes;
        // this number decides how wrong the player may be **perpendicular** to the
        // blade — and in a real horizontal flight past a nape that axis is
        // **VERTICAL**. Measured (`docs/FINDINGS.md` FIND-147): the half-window is
        // `cortex_radius_m + thickness_m`, so at 0.12 a player had to put his EYE
        // within **±0.28 m** of a scuttler's nape and ±0.67 m of a husk's while
        // travelling 21 m/s. That is a third of a tick of vertical error, and it is
        // invisible: you fly the line, you press, and nothing happens.
        //
        // At 0.30 the same windows are ±0.50 m and ±0.85 m. It also buys the last
        // 0.15 m the `large` class needed to reach `scripts/f030-hitbox.txt`'s 0.60 m
        // cushion, which `reach_m` alone could not give without making the cast a
        // spear (the drawn pair is 0.93 m of steel — `FIND-127`).
        //
        // **Why this and not more `reach_m`.** A slash is a swept arc and a real one
        // is a swath, not a wire; 0.60 m of swath from a pair of blades is a picture
        // a player can believe, where a 2.15 m blade on a 1.80 m body is not. It is
        // also the reference's own vocabulary — AoT:R tunes a "player slash hitbox
        // size" by percentage and never speaks of blade length
        // (`docs/gameplay/references.md`).
        //
        // ⚠️ It is direction-blind like every other length here: it lengthens the
        // reach towards the titan's face too. `titan.ron: cortex_half_angle_deg` is
        // what keeps that honest.
        thickness_m: 0.20,
        // The whole swing. `active_from_s`..`active_to_s` is the part that cuts —
        // without them the blade cuts during its own wind-up, which is the shortcut
        // that makes a slash feel like a hitbox.
        swing_s: 0.35,
        active_from_s: 0.08,
        active_to_s: 0.22,
        // Without a cooldown the slash is autofire.
        cooldown_s: 0.30,
    ),

    // F-034 hit stop and the camera kick. ⚠️ UNTUNED — and note that F-034 had NO
    // numbers at all anywhere in the repository before this line.
    //
    // The number that forces this block to exist: a husk cortex is 1.10 m across and
    // a player crosses it at 30 m/s in **36.7 ms = 2.2 frames**. Without a stop the
    // kill happens and the player sees a counter change, not a kill. 0.12 s makes it
    // about 9.4 frames.
    //
    // Seconds in the file, TICKS in the code: the hit stop is counted in fixed ticks
    // and never in wall-clock time (shared::HitStop). The conversion is
    // `round(s * simulation_hz)` and it happens once, at the boundary.
    feel: (
        hit_stop_cortex_s: 0.12,
        // A non-lethal hit stops much less, or every scratch reads like a kill.
        hit_stop_normal_s: 0.04,
        camera_kick_deg: 3.5,
        camera_kick_s: 0.18,

        // F-043 — the hit mark above the crosshair. ⚠️ UNTUNED, both invented on 2026-08-19.
        //
        // **0.0 switches the whole element off** and that is the row's "vollstaendig
        // abschaltbar", answered here instead of in a settings screen. It is not a disabled
        // feature hiding behind a flag: the node stays spawned, `step_flash` simply never
        // gives it anything to say (src/hud/hit_mark.rs).
        //
        // 0.55 s is longer than `hit_stop_cortex_s` (0.12) plus `camera_kick_s` (0.18), on
        // purpose — the word has to still be there when the image starts moving again, or the
        // player reads it during the freeze and never connects it to the swing.
        hit_mark_s: 0.55,
        // Where CUT stops and GRAZE starts. `blades.min_speed_m_s` (8.0) is the floor below
        // which no hit is reported at all, so this number splits the band 8.0 .. ~40 that a
        // player actually produces. 18.0 puts the measured pass of `scripts/f032-swords.txt`
        // (20.67 m/s out of an 11.20 m fall) on the CUT side — a fall that deliberate should
        // not read as a scratch — and leaves the whole run-up-and-poke range below it.
        strong_hit_m_s: 18.0,
    ),
    // What a resupply point of the main building gives back, per second of standing at it.
    // **Where** the points are is `missions.ron: hub`, what they DO is here (§4).
    //
    // ⚠️ UNTUNED, all four. The gas pair is from 2026-08-12; the blade pair landed with the
    // headquarters and `F-033 Klingenhaltbarkeit`, which had no number anywhere in the
    // repository before this line — the same hole `feel` above was opened for.
    resupply: (
        gas_per_s: 40.0,
        range_m: 4.0,

        // A fresh pair every two thirds of a second. It is deliberately NOT instant: standing
        // at the rack has to be a decision with a duration, the same way refuelling is, or the
        // supply run stops being a run. Five pairs from empty is 3.3 s.
        //
        // Fractional on purpose — the arithmetic runs on a float `charge` and only whole pairs
        // ever reach `Blades::pairs_left`. A rate of 1.0 would hide that the accumulator
        // exists, and it is the part that has to be right at 60 Hz.
        blade_pairs_per_s: 1.5,

        // The pair in the harness is honed back to 1.0 sharpness at this rate. Faster than the
        // pairs come, because a half-blunt blade is the thing you fix before you top the
        // harness up — 0.0 to 1.0 takes half a second.
        sharpen_per_s: 2.0,
    ),
)
